# Prisma migrations are generated, immutable, and checksummed by Prisma.
# Their bytes must stay identical across the public repo and downstream forks,
# so git must not flag (or try to auto-fix) whitespace in them — e.g. a
# trailing blank line at EOF would otherwise trip `git diff --check` on merge.
#
# `eol=lf` is what actually delivers that "identical bytes" promise. Every blob
# on `main` is already LF, but this was the one tracked text class left
# unpinned, so a Windows checkout (`core.autocrlf=true`) silently materialised
# CRLF while the LF-pinned `*.ts` seed source stayed LF. That made
# `page-content-starter-backfill.test.ts` compare an LF template literal
# against CRLF file bytes and fail on Windows only — green on Linux CI, so it
# was repeatedly written off as an environment flake. It also left Prisma
# migration checksums platform-dependent. Pinning is zero-churn (the blobs are
# already LF) and ends both problems.
prisma/migrations/**/*.sql text eol=lf -whitespace

# ESM scripts are imported by vitest tests as well as run via `node`. A CRLF
# checkout (Windows autocrlf) of a shebang line breaks vite-node's import of
# the module, so these always materialise with LF.
scripts/*.mjs text eol=lf

# The schema is hand-edited (AGENTS.md "Change Discipline") and is the file most
# likely to be ported between the public repo and its forks. A Windows editor
# silently rewriting it to CRLF turns a 14-line change into a ~9,400-line diff
# that conflicts on every line at the next port (#2129), so pin it to LF.
prisma/schema.prisma text eol=lf

# The hand-edited markdown carries the same hazard as the schema, and `AGENTS.md`
# / `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` / `CHANGELOG.md` are the files most often ported
# between the public repo and its forks. A Windows editor rewriting one to CRLF
# turned a 28-line change into an 848-line diff, destroyed blame, and made
# `git diff --check` flag every line as trailing whitespace (#2162). Pin the
# whole set to LF rather than waiting to relearn this per file.
*.md text eol=lf

# Source files carry the same hazard, and it has now bitten three times: the
# schema (#2129), the markdown above (#2162), and two source files on the
# #2160 branch — where a CRLF flip in a test file was independently read as
# "harmless re-indentation" by three separate reviews, because `git diff -w`
# ignores whitespace INCLUDING the carriage return and so reports clean on
# exactly the churn that matters. Every tracked source file on `main` is
# already LF, so pinning the set is zero-churn today and ends the class.
*.ts text eol=lf
# The main-branch blob for this file contains a historical literal NUL in the
# resolution-key separator. Force textual review while this change normalises
# it to the equivalent escaped source form.
src/lib/config-transfer/import-types.ts text eol=lf diff
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
# #2687: complete the LF pins for the file-size ratchet's source-extension
# scope. Git warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on the probe files used to
# prove the classifier hole — the same Windows-only split that took days to
# diagnose for the migration SQL and the shell scripts above.
*.mts text eol=lf
*.cts text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# #2686: the secret scanner's own two config files were the remaining tracked
# text class with no pin, and both are parsed in ways CRLF breaks. gitleaks
# reads `.gitleaksignore` a line at a time and compares each line to a finding
# fingerprint, so a trailing `\r` makes an entry match nothing and the required
# `Secret scan (gitleaks)` check goes red over a finding somebody already
# reviewed and suppressed. `.toml` is pinned as a class rather than by name, so
# a second TOML file added later inherits it.
*.toml text eol=lf
.gitleaksignore text eol=lf

# #2687: tracked plain-text data files are the same class again. Several are
# read line by line and compared byte-for-byte, so a CRLF checkout would fail on
# Windows and pass on Linux CI — the exact split that took days to diagnose for
# the migration SQL (#2399) and the shell scripts. Live examples, because a rule
# whose only example has been deleted is a rule nobody can check:
# `src/lib/__tests__/support/email-render-pins.txt` is compared line by line
# against rendered output by `email-render-equivalence.test.ts`, and
# `scripts/data-migration-verification-grandfathered.txt` is parsed by
# `check-data-migration-verification.sh`, which gates a required check.
# (#2979 deleted the file-size baseline this comment used to cite; the rule
# outlived it, which is why it is pinned as a class rather than by name.) The
# class pin also means the next tracked `.txt` inherits it; the existing tracked
# `.txt` blobs are already LF, so this is zero churn.
*.txt text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the fourth instance of the same class, and the worst of
# them, because the failure does not look like a line-ending problem at all.
# Every tracked `*.sh` here opens with `set -Eeuo pipefail`. Materialised as
# CRLF on a Windows checkout, bash reads the option name as `pipefail\r` and
# dies on that line with `set: pipefail: invalid option name` — pointing at
# the shell, not at the file. So `scripts/check-migration-safety-coverage.sh`
# and `scripts/validate-blue-green-migrations.sh` exit 2 before reading a
# single migration, and `review-findings-contracts.test.ts`, which shells out
# to both, fails 33 assertions on every Windows checkout and inside any Linux
# container fed from one. That suite was written off as "environmental, slow
# MSYS process spawn" for days; it was this. A permanently-red suite is worse
# than a broken one, because it trains people to ignore failures — and it very
# nearly hid a real regression on a money path (#2308).
#
# Same fix and same zero churn as the migration SQL (#2399): the stored blobs
# are already LF, so this only stops Windows re-materialising them as CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The blue/green migration-safety ledger is the fifth instance of the same
# class, and the one where byte-level trust matters most: every row is parsed by
# `scripts/check-migration-safety-coverage.sh` and hand-audited, and its whole
# value is that its bytes can be trusted across the public repo and its forks. It
# was the last tracked text class left without an `eol=lf` pin, so it rode on
# `core.autocrlf` and git warned on every Windows touch (flagged in #2431's fix
# round). Same fix and same zero churn as the migration SQL and shell scripts —
# the stored blob is already LF; this only stops Windows re-materialising CRLF.
docs/BLUE_GREEN_MIGRATION_SAFETY.tsv text eol=lf

# The rendered-email pin file (#2689) is the sixth instance of the same class.
# Its parser strips a stray CR defensively, so a CRLF checkout no longer makes
# the gate cry wolf. The committed file is still a byte-level review contract,
# though: leaving it on `core.autocrlf` would make a Windows regeneration rewrite
# every one of its 219 entries and obscure the intended pin changes. Keep it LF so
# review diffs and generated bytes are platform-independent. Zero churn: the
# stored blob is already LF.
src/lib/__tests__/support/email-render-pins.txt text eol=lf

# CHANGELOG.md: every concurrent lane prepends its entry to `## Unreleased`,
# so plain three-way merge conflicts on nearly every rebase and every
# update-from-main — all session long, on every agent stream. `union` keeps
# BOTH sides' added lines automatically, which is exactly the resolution every
# one of those conflicts got by hand. Caveat: on a release-cut commit (moving
# Unreleased under a version header) union can concatenate two restructurings
# silently — eyeball the CHANGELOG on release PRs.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
